00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.359
Content Delivery Network - Transcript

00:00:01.359 --> 00:00:03.800
Hi, I'm Ryan Sumner I am a Chief Delivery
Network Architect with IBM Cloud and today

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.000
I'm going help you answer what is a Content
Delivery Network.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.170
So, in short, a Content Delivery Network,
or CDN, is a service that accelerates internet

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.170
content delivery.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:22.050
So, in other words the main benefit of a CDN
is that it makes your website faster.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:28.050
So, before I get into describing to you how
it accomplishes that and some of the other

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.050
benefits.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.620
First, I want to talk to you about some of
the challenges that we have where we have

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.210
users all around the world, but we don't have
servers all around the world and the experience

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.060
that those users have due to that dynamic.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:47.090
So, I got a simple diagram here showing a
server hosted down in Dallas, this is my website,

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.829
and I have users all around the world.

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:56.310
So, in Sydney I might have five, in London
I've got five, New York I might have ten,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.710
and LA I might have ten.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.440
I’ve thirty users around the world they're
accessing my server and my website down in

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.440
Dallas.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.360
So, lets you can follow a set of these users
in their journey and let's look at their users

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.250
down in Sydney.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.140
They make a request to the website they got
an eighty six hundred mile hike to Dallas

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.350
and then in eighty-six hundred mile hike back.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:24.330
The amount of time that that takes is usually
measured in milliseconds and just that round

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:30.580
trip might be about a hundred and seventy
milliseconds.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:37.310
For our users up in London that might be about
a hundred milliseconds.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:44.770
Our users in New York City can probably experience
about a forty milliseconds round-trip time.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.270
And over in LA about thirty.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:55.030
So, as you can see, the further you’re away,
the longer it takes and ultimately the slower

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.829
the website will be for you.

00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:04.400
So, this is where the CDN comes into play
and this is how it actually accomplishes the

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.450
increase in speed which is by reducing the
amount of distance between the user and the

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:12.239
content, or the server providing the content.

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:18.590
So, what it does by doing that it places these
Content Delivery Network end points in as

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:22.440
many locations around the world as possible.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.250
And in our case, we're going to assume we've
got one in just about every location where

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.390
users exist.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:37.040
So, now when the user in Sydney, or London,
or New York City, or LA tries to access some

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:43.200
content its first retrieved by the Content
Delivery Network service and then distributed

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.200
around the world.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.629
So, we have a single request down to the Dallas
server.

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:49.650
It's now the distributed all around the world.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:54.849
And our users in London now instead of going
all the way to Dallas they're able to retrieve

00:02:54.849 --> 00:03:02.310
that content directly from their closest geographical
location, drastically reducing the amount

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.970
of time that it takes to retrieve that content.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:12.250
So, as you can see here it's a very basic
how a CDN is able to provide the benefits

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:16.489
of to the end user by reducing the amount
of time that it takes to deliver the service,

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:21.940
but what you're not seen here is an indirect
benefit is the reduction in the amount of

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.200
traffic that actually hits the Dallas server.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:34.050
So, the indirect benefit is that you actually
see a reduction in the load, or the reduction

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:38.640
in the amount of capacity that you need in
Dallas to serve all these users.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:45.010
So, another indirect benefit because there
is much less validity and so much less stuff

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:49.540
happening in Dallas, because all these users
are not having to make these trips, and I'm

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.340
also not having to communicate with users
so far away.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:04:00.209
The Dallas environment may also see an increase
in up time.

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:04.489
And then lastly because the users are not
really directly communicating with the server

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:13.080
down in Dallas you have the indirect benefit
of an increase in security through obscurity.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:20.250
So, it's pretty basic to understand how a
CDN works, in the end it provide a better

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.029
benefit to the end user.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.409
Thanks for watching this overview of Content
Delivery Network.

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:28.409
If you liked it, or have any questions please
comment below.

